#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055984 :tutorial/content "To solve a radical equation where the radical is isolated on one side of the equation, like\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x+1} = 3\n$$\n\nwe square both sides. Remember that squaring is the opposite of the square root, so\n\n$$\n\\left(\\sqrt{x+1}\\right)^2 =x+1\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\sqrt{x + 1})^{2} &= 3^{2} \\\\\nx + 1 &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the equation in the standard way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 1 &= 9 \\\\\nx &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{(8) + 1} &= ^(?)3 \\\\\n\\sqrt{9} &= ^(?)3 \\\\\n3 &= 3✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=8$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x+1}=3\n$$\n\nis $x=8$." :tutorial/id #uuid "60edc96d-ba13-5542-b95d-c7e13ced8006" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3418 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055985 :tutorial/content "Some radical equations have no solutions. For example, consider the radical equation\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3-x} = -2\n$$\n\nThe square root of a number is always positive or zero. So in the equation above, the right-hand side is negative, while the left-hand side is always positive or zero. Consequently the two sides will never be equal, and there is no real solution.\n\nWe could also determine that there is no solution by using the same process as usual. If we try to solve this equation by squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{3 - x} &= -2 \\\\\n(\\sqrt{3 - x})^{2} &= (-2)^{2} \\\\\n3 - x &= 4 \\\\\nx &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, when we plug this result into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{3 - (-1)} &= ^(?) - 2 \\\\\n\\sqrt{4} &= ^(?) - 2 \\\\\n&= 2 \\mid \\ne - 2\\cdot\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=-1$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, so we *cannot* accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3-x}=-2\n$$\n\nhas no real solution." :tutorial/id #uuid "c14f3a94-fdf5-5c7a-9929-101b5d933468" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3455 :tutorial/title "Radical Equations with No Solutions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055941 :tutorial/content "When we want to solve a square root radical equation which has the square root on the left-hand side and a linear function on the right-hand side, like\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2x-1}=x\n$$\n\nwe raise both sides to the power of $2$. This allows us to get rid of the radical, and gives us a quadratic equation that is easier to solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\sqrt{2x - 1})^{2} &= (x)^{2} \\\\\n2x - 1 &= x^{2} \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that in square root equations, we should always check the obtained value(s) to ensure that they are actually solutions of the original equation.\n\nHere, we check that the obtained value $x=1$ is actually a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{2(1) - 1} &= ^(?)(1) \\\\\n\\sqrt{1} &= 1^(?) \\\\\n1 &= 1✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2x-1}=x\n$$\n\nhas the solution $x=1$." :tutorial/id #uuid "d17df195-3553-5a81-ac34-282046536fa2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14245 :tutorial/title "Equations Containing Additional Linear Terms"}]}
